WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.170 --> 00:00:06.330
When you write a novel, you have to engage in the process of

00:00:06.330 --> 00:00:11.820
world-building. And you have all
these linked systems in reality.

00:00:11.820 --> 00:00:16.740
We have a reality, of course,
but in the New York City

00:00:16.740 --> 00:00:22.830
reality of Kim Stanley Robinson's New
York 2140—this is the cover art from

00:00:22.830 --> 00:00:29.369
that book—and sea levels have
risen by 50 years in a very short period

00:00:29.369 --> 00:00:34.170
of time. What does this mean if
they've gone up by 50 feet, for a city

00:00:34.170 --> 00:00:38.940
that's on average 33 feet above sea
level? What does this mean? Well, you're

00:00:38.940 --> 00:00:42.450
gonna have this 'super Venice,' as he
calls it, right? You've got

00:00:42.450 --> 00:00:47.340
flooding, permanent flooding, up to about
46th Street. And what does that mean for

00:00:47.340 --> 00:00:53.160
how people move about the city? What does
that mean in a rapidly

00:00:53.160 --> 00:00:57.300
decarbonizing economy where you don't
have long-distance transport that's as

00:00:57.300 --> 00:00:59.670
easy? What does that mean for food
production? Where do people get their

00:00:59.670 --> 00:01:03.690
vegetables from? Where do people get
their produce from? What does it mean for

00:01:03.690 --> 00:01:09.990
the governance of small scale
organizations like an apartment co-op in

00:01:09.990 --> 00:01:15.080
New York City, right? These are the types
of consequences. You have a global change,

00:01:15.080 --> 00:01:21.540
the effects are felt
locally, right? And you have to play out

00:01:21.540 --> 00:01:28.170
how all these things are going to work,
right? It's a model, it's Kim

00:01:28.170 --> 00:01:32.880
Stanley Robinson's opinion of what's
going to happen. But I don't know, I think

00:01:32.880 --> 00:01:39.299
he's on to something in most of these
things. I know that Annalee Newitz spoke

00:01:39.299 --> 00:01:48.329
recently about—what was she talking
about? Science needs fiction. And she had

00:01:48.329 --> 00:01:53.310
such a fabulous example of how you have
 to come to grips with the

00:01:53.310 --> 00:01:56.759
consequences of your narrative arc.
Okay, so she's got this pirate in

00:01:56.759 --> 00:02:01.890
the Arctic, right, who's on the internet a
lot on a shipboard. And then she's like,

00:02:01.890 --> 00:02:06.030
"Oh god, where does she get the internet
from," right? And so she had to invent

00:02:06.030 --> 00:02:09.300
something, a technology that
provides Internet in the Arctic.

00:02:09.300 --> 00:02:12.630
And this is the
notion. You have to flesh out these

00:02:12.630 --> 00:02:18.480
worlds; you're playing with complex
systems. It's actually not a huge point—I

00:02:18.480 --> 00:02:21.390
mean, well it's an important point—
but it doesn't need to be

00:02:21.390 --> 00:02:29.160
belabored. This is fan art
for Paolo Bacigalupi's

00:02:29.160 --> 00:02:33.600
'The Windup Girl.' And in a
decarbonized world, supply chains are

00:02:33.600 --> 00:02:38.730
going to be highly altered, right? Where do you get your food from?

00:02:38.730 --> 00:02:42.620
Where's the energy to run manufacturing?
Where does that come from?

00:02:42.620 --> 00:02:51.740
Or if you have an industrial plant that
produces the machines that

00:02:51.740 --> 00:02:56.580
you're trying to build, where do you get
the power to run your machinery?

00:02:56.580 --> 00:03:02.190
These are all things that come out in
'The Windup Girl.' So that's point number

00:03:02.190 --> 00:03:08.160
one. Point number two has to do with
imagining the future and imagining

00:03:08.160 --> 00:03:13.290
better worlds. And again there's this
terrific Mohsin Hamid quote: "Part of the

00:03:13.290 --> 00:03:16.830
great political crisis we face in the
world today is a failure to imagine

00:03:16.830 --> 00:03:22.100
plausible desirable futures. We are
surrounded by nostalgic visions,

00:03:22.100 --> 00:03:28.470
violently nostalgic visions. Fiction can
imagine differently. I think this is an

00:03:28.470 --> 00:03:34.620
enormously important idea. And I feel
like it probably resonates with anyone

00:03:34.620 --> 00:03:40.170
who is dissatisfied, say, with the current
administration and politics in America.

00:03:40.170 --> 00:03:44.490
We really are feeling this incredibly
violently nostalgic moment.

00:03:44.490 --> 00:03:50.040
And typically these nostalgias are for
things that really didn't happen, or they

00:03:50.040 --> 00:03:53.400
certainly didn't happen the way we
remember them. Memory is an

00:03:53.400 --> 00:04:00.750
imperfect process. And so by writing
fiction, we can imagine a better world.

00:04:00.750 --> 00:04:04.680
And I think this actually has really
important implications for the type of

00:04:04.680 --> 00:04:10.650
fiction that we should write. And in
particular—I took all the slides out

00:04:10.650 --> 00:04:14.010
of all the ridiculous movies that have
dealt with climate—but there are lots of

00:04:14.010 --> 00:04:18.270
them. You don't have to think very
hard about it. And what's the

00:04:18.270 --> 00:04:21.600
commonality of them? They're all
post-apocalyptic. We

00:04:21.600 --> 00:04:28.230
love Mad Max and Waterworld—we didn't love Waterworld, but we loved

00:04:28.230 --> 00:04:32.640
the idea of it.
These are post-apocalyptic

00:04:32.640 --> 00:04:40.440
stories. And they're not better futures.
Maybe there's some heroine, you know, Katniss

00:04:40.440 --> 00:04:44.430
Everdeen or whoever comes in
and does the heroic thing, and

00:04:44.430 --> 00:04:47.850
that's interesting. But it's not—
we're not imagining a better world. I

00:04:47.850 --> 00:04:52.620
think we need to do better in imagining
better worlds. And the thing is, there's

00:04:52.620 --> 00:04:56.340
plenty of fiction that does exactly that.
So that's the good news. Now we have to

00:04:56.340 --> 00:05:07.829
convince Hollywood and whoever makes TV shows.

